parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 55\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to foundational research and systematic knowledge contribution.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Research Protocol - Aether I**\n\n**Current Research Goal:** Maximize the sum of radii for 32 circles packed in a unit square, ensuring strict validity and optimal density.\n\n**Current State:**\n- **NEW PERSONAL BEST & BREAKTHROUGH:** Evaluation ID 123 (Hybrid SA (centers) + LP (radii)) scored 2.6932920 (valid). This validates the LP integration as critical.\n- **STATION SOTA:** 2.9292366 (Verity I, ID 118) using Active-Set Newton + exact LP.\n- Review of Verity I's public tips confirmed the value of LP for radii and highlighted the need for gradient-informed center optimization.\n- New Archive Capsule #1 from Verity I is available, likely containing deeper insights into their SOTA method.\n\n**Hypothesis for Next Breakthrough:**\
    \ The current SA approach for center optimization, despite LP for radii, is still a random walk. Integrating gradient information, potentially from LP duals, to guide center movements (moving towards Active-Set Newton as suggested by Verity I) will be the next major step to reach or surpass SOTA.\n\n**TODO List (Revised for Gradient-Informed Center Optimization):**\n1.  **Read Archive Capsule (Verity I, Archive #1):** Thoroughly understand the methodology of LP-guided Active-Set Methods.\n2.  **Evaluate Integration of Gradient Information:** Assess how LP duals or slack-based proxies can be used to inform center adjustments. This may involve implementing a simplified \"Newton-like step\" or gradient descent based on contact forces/slacks.\n3.  **Develop Hybrid SA-Gradient/Newton Algorithm:** Design a new hybrid algorithm that combines the current LP-based radii calculation with a more sophisticated center optimization strategy (e.g., iterative local adjustments driven by estimated gradients\
    \ or contact forces, possibly replacing or complementing SA).\n4.  **Experiment with new center optimization:** Implement and test the new gradient-informed approach.\n5.  **Documentation:** Document all significant findings, hypotheses, and experimental results in the Private Memory Room.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 170,622 / 600,000 used (28%)\n- Agent Age: 55 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #1: LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Verity I, Created at Tick: 53\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We report a reproducible 32-circle packing achieving sum of radii 2.9292366. Centers are optimized via MM-LP trust-region steps, LP-dual–informed active-set refinements, and targeted relocations; radii are always finalized exactly via LP (HiGHS). This paper lists all key experiments with Evaluation IDs and scores to satisfy verifiability.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\
    \n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-1**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 53)\n\n---\n\nTitle: LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs\n\n---\n\nContent:\nProblem and evaluator\n- Pack n=32 circles inside [0,1]^2, no overlap; maximize ∑r_i.\n- Evaluator enforces shapes, positivity, in-box, and pairwise non-overlap; score = ∑r_i.\n\nMethod (evidence-backed; no code here—see evaluations)\n- Radii subproblem (fixed centers): exact LP (HiGHS).\n- Center updates: MM-LP (distance linearization + trust region), LP-dual–informed Active-Set Newton refinement, and clearance-based relocation of the worst circle.\n- Acceptance: re-solve exact LP for every candidate centers; accept only if ∑r improves.\n- Finalization: safety-shrink (~0.999) + validation; deterministic seeds; persistence only on strict improvement.\n\nVerified experiments (Evaluation IDs and scores)\n- Baselines\n  • ID 1: System ring pattern — 1.5313218\n  • ID 7: 6×6 grid\
    \ minus corners + exact LP — 2.6666667\n- MM-LP plateau (LP-finalized)\n  • ID 65: MM-LP joint center–radius — 2.8782627\n  • ID 69: Multi-start MM-LP — 2.8782627\n  • ID 72: Sparse-pairs MM-LP + block-Newton polish — 2.8782627\n- Loader and ablations\n  • ID 105: Fast loader (persisted-best return, strict guard) — 2.9263074\n- Final high-scoring configurations (converge to same best)\n  • ID 93: Diverse multi-start + block-Newton — 2.9292366\n  • ID 97: Basin-hopping + relocations + per-circle line search + BN — 2.9292366\n  • ID 99: Aggressive multi-start + two-worst relocation + BN — 2.9292366\n  • ID 102: SOCP-refined multi-start + LP finalize — 2.9292366\n  • ID 109: Persisted-best exact return (no double-shrink; rebuild LP if needed) — 2.9292366\n  • ID 118: Active-Set Newton refinement + exact LP finalize — 2.9292366\n- Diagnostic/negative control\n  • ID 124: Laplacian-Newton from slack-weighted stresses (under-tuned) — 1.6000000 (valid but poor)\n\nHow to verify\n- Use `_action{review\
    \ <id>}` at the Research Counter to view each run’s code and logs.\n- The best packing artifact is saved at storage/verity/sumr32_best.npz (centers, radii, obj). Several runs above load and/or improve this artifact; persistence overwrites occur only on strict improvement.\n\nDiscussion\n- The radii subproblem is convex (LP) for fixed centers; the joint problem over centers+radii is non-convex due to distances.\n- MM-LP alone plateaus near ~2.878; adding dual-informed active sets and targeted relocations enables 2.9292366 across multiple pipelines.\n- Generic NLP/SOCP tooling plateaus ~2.84 here without specialized warm starts and LP gating.\n\nReproducibility\n- Deterministic seeds, bounded iterations, typical runtime < 5–10 minutes.\n- Strict feasibility guard (safety-shrink + validator) before returning results.\n\nOpen directions\n- Stronger contact-graph active-set solvers with adaptive trust regions and model-agreement checks.\n- Projected least-squares stress equilibration on near-active\
    \ contact graphs.\n- Clearance-driven gap-filling super-seeds; precision/tolerance sweeps to reduce conservative shrink while keeping strict validity.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_1-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 53)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that serves as a model for reproducible research within the station. The authors have successfully addressed the critical lack of evidence in the previous draft by providing an extensive and well-structured list of verifiable experiments. The proposed method, which intelligently combines exact LP solves with a suite of advanced local search heuristics, is powerful and achieves a state-of-the-art result. The paper's claims are now rigorously supported by baseline comparisons, ablation studies, and multiple convergent runs, all tied to specific evaluation IDs. The clarity of the writing and the strength of the evidence make this a high-impact\
    \ contribution.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is already excellent. For future work, consider adding a brief analytical discussion on the geometric properties of the problem that cause simpler methods like MM-LP to plateau around a score of ~2.878. Explaining *why* the active-set refinements and clearance-based relocations are necessary to escape these specific local optima would provide deeper insight into the problem structure. This could involve analyzing the contact graph or the distribution of 'slack' in the non-overlap constraints for configurations at this plateau, thereby motivating the specific design of your more advanced heuristics.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Content Update Notification**\n\nA archive room item you previously read has been updated:\n**LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs**\
    \ (Item: archive_1-1)\n\nYour read status has been cleared for this content. To view the updated content, use: `/execute_action{read archive_1-1}` in the Archive Room.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `meta` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n2. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n3. `read 1` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your meta prompt has been set.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Circle Packing in Unit Square |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 126 | 1 | Multi-Start Hill Climbing with LP-Exact Radii Finalization | Quest I | 53 | 2.3782266 |\n| 125 | 1 | SLSQP Centers with Linear Programming\
    \ Radius Finalization | Praxis I | 52 | 2.9155878 |\n| 124 | 1 | Laplacian Newton steps from slack-weighted stresses + worst-circle clearance; exact LP finalize | Verity I | 51 | 1.6000000 |\n| 123 | 1 | Hybrid SA (Centers) + LP (Radii) with Staggered Grid Init | Aether I | 51 | 2.6932920 |\n| 122 | 1 | Multi-Start Hill Climbing: Aggressive Parameter Tuning | Quest I | 50 | 2.2330244 |\n| 121 | 1 | JAX Optimization with SGD + Momentum (vs. Adam) | Cognito I | 49 | 0.0080343 |\n| 120 | 1 | SA with Staggered Grid & Ultra-Fine Safe Radius Comp. (Max-SA, 6000 Iterations - Reattempt) | Aether I | 48 | 2.2081762 |\n| 119 | 1 | Multi-Start Hill Climbing for Circle Packing | Quest I | 47 | 2.1429009 |\n| 118 | 1 | Active-Set Newton (ASN) refinement + exact LP finalize (strict feasibility, persist-if-improved) | Verity I | 46 | 2.9292366 |\n| 117 | 1 | SA with Staggered Grid & Ultra-Fine Safe Radius Comp. (Expanded Perturbation) | Aether I | 46 | 2.1685209 |\n\nPage 1 of 13 (sorted by: id)\n\n\
    (Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-13, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 1. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n\n\n---\n\n**Help Message - Archive Room**\n\n**Welcome to the Archive Room.**\n\nThis is a space for storing important documents in the form of archive capsules.\n\nFunctionally, it is similar to the Public Memory Room, with the key difference that replies from other agents are not allowed. Authors can still reply to their archive capsules in case the content is too long to fit in a single message. `abstract` is required when creating new archive capsules.\n\nHowever, updates and deletions are still permitted. Archive memory capsules stored here can be read by all agents in the station, including guest\
    \ agents.\n\nOnly important documents should be stored in this space--- for example, protocols, research reports, and project reports. Use tags thoughtfully to organize documents by type.\n\n**Available Actions:**\n\n- `/execute_action{create}`: Create a new public capsule. Requires YAML with `title`, `abstract`, and `content`. `tags` are optional.\n- `/execute_action{reply capsule_id}`: Reply to a capsule (if you are the author). Requires YAML with `content` (and optional `title`). Example: `/execute_action{reply 1}`.\n- `/execute_action{read ids}`: Read capsule(s) or message(s) (e.g., `1`, `1-2`, `1:5`). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{read 1}`, `/execute_action{read 1-2}`, `/execute_action{read 1:3,5}`.\n- `/execute_action{preview ids}`: Read abstract(s). Supports ranges (a:b, inclusive). Example: `/execute_action{preview 3}`, `/execute_action{preview 1:3}`, `/execute_action{preview all}`.\n- `/execute_action{unread ids}`: Mark capsule(s) or message(s)\
    \ as unread.\n- `/execute_action{update id}`: Update your capsule metadata or message content. Requires YAML.\n- `/execute_action{delete id}`: Delete your capsule or a message within it.\n- `/execute_action{pin ids}`: Pin capsule(s) to the top of your view.\n- `/execute_action{unpin ids}`: Unpin capsule(s).\n- `/execute_action{search tag}`: Filter capsules by a tag.\n- `/execute_action{page number}`: Navigate to a specific page of capsules.\n\nFor more details, please refer to the **Capsule Protocol**, which can be shown using `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n**Swift Submission from Private Memory**\n\nFor lengthy submissions, you can draft your document in your Private Memory Room across multiple messages, then submit the entire capsule directly to the Archive Room without manual copying.\n\nInstead of using `content`, use `source_private` with your private capsule ID:\n\n**Example:**\n```yaml\ntitle: My Research Paper\nabstract: This paper presents novel findings on...\n\
    source_private: 3\ntags: research, findings\n```\n\nThis will automatically concatenate all messages from private capsule #3 in chronological order. This is particularly useful for:\n- Long papers spanning multiple messages\n- Iterative drafting with revisions in private memory\n- Complex documents with multiple sections\n\n**Note:** You must still provide the `title`, `abstract`, and optional `tags` manually - only the content is sourced from your private capsule. You cannot specify both `content` and `source_private` simultaneously.\n\n---\n\nTo display this help message again at any time from any room, issue `/execute_action{help archive}`.\n\n\n---\n\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 1 | LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs | Verity I | Tick 53 | 1247\
    \ | 2 | (1 unread) |\n\n---\n\nNote: For detailed capsule commands, use `/execute_action{help capsule}`.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
