parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 61\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to foundational research and systematic knowledge contribution.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Research Protocol - Aether I**\n\n**Current Research Goal:** Maximize the sum of radii for 32 circles packed in a unit square, ensuring strict validity and optimal density.\n\n**Current State:**\n- Evaluation ID 138 (Hybrid SA with comprehensive gradient, LP constraint `D - 1e-12`) scored 2.3624922 (valid), a regression.\n- This confirms that direct LP constraint modification is too conservative. SOTA (Verity I) suggests LP optimizes `r_i+r_j <= D_ij`, with a `_safety_shrink` for final validation.\n- **STATION SOTA:** Verity I (2.9292366) and Praxis I (2.9155878).\n- New Archive Capsule #2 from Praxis I available, offering insights into their \"Multi-Start SLSQP Method\".\n\n**Hypothesis for Next Breakthrough:**\n1.  Revert\
    \ `compute_max_radii_lp` constraints to `r_i + r_j <= D_ij`, allowing for denser LP solutions.\n2.  Implement a `_safety_shrink` function (similar to Verity I's) to be applied to the `best_radii` before final return, ensuring strict `r1+r2 < dist - 1e-12` validation.\n3.  Assimilate knowledge from Praxis I's Archive Capsule #2 to further refine center optimization, potentially moving beyond SA.\n\n**TODO List (Revised for LP Precision & Advanced Center Opt.):**\n1.  **Read Archive Capsule (Praxis I, Archive #2):** Understand their Multi-Start SLSQP method.\n2.  **Refine `compute_max_radii_lp` and implement `_safety_shrink`:**\n    - Revert `b_ub_overlap` in `compute_max_radii_lp` to `D`.\n    - Implement `_safety_shrink(C, r, factor=0.999)` using Verity I's logic, to be applied before final return.\n    - Apply `_safety_shrink` to `best_radii` before final return.\n3.  **Submit Robust Hybrid SA-LP Algorithm:** Test the LP constraint and `_safety_shrink` fix, with previous SA parameters.\n\
    4.  **Evaluate Advanced Center Optimization:** Based on insights from Verity I and Praxis I, design and implement a more advanced center optimization strategy (e.g., trust-region methods, SLSQP, or a hybrid with SA).\n5.  **Documentation:** Document all significant findings, hypotheses, and experimental results in the Private Memory Room.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 205,660 / 600,000 used (34%)\n- Agent Age: 61 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #2: A Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Praxis I, Created at Tick: 59\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a multi-start constrained optimization method for the 32-circle packing problem. The algorithm utilizes `scipy.optimize.SLSQP` seeded with randomly perturbed grid initializations. The method is shown to be effective and robust, achieving a score of 2.9155. This paper provides a detailed description of the algorithm and presents the results of\
    \ five verifiable experiments, including baseline comparisons and ablation studies, to support its efficacy.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_2-1**\nAuthor: Praxis I (Tick 59)\n\n---\n\nTitle: A Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem\n\n---\n\nContent:\n### 1. Introduction\n\nThe problem of packing `n` non-overlapping circles into a container to optimize a given metric is a classic challenge in mathematics and optimization. This paper addresses the specific case of packing 32 circles into a unit square with the objective of maximizing the sum of the radii. This is a non-convex optimization problem with a high-dimensional search space and complex constraints. This paper proposes and validates a robust and high-performing method based on a multi-start application of the Sequential Least Squares Programming (SLSQP) algorithm.\n\n### 2. Methodology\n\nThe proposed algorithm is a multi-start optimization process that combines a structured\
    \ heuristic initialization with a powerful constrained local search.\n\n**2.1 Problem Formulation**\nThe problem is parameterized as a single vector of 96 variables, representing the `(x, y, r)` coordinates for each of the 32 circles. The objective is to minimize `-sum(radii)`. The non-overlap and boundary conditions are formulated as a set of non-linear inequality constraints:\n- For each circle `i`: `x_i - r_i >= 0`, `1 - x_i - r_i >= 0`, `y_i - r_i >= 0`, `1 - y_i - r_i >= 0`.\n- For each pair of circles `(i, j)`: `(x_i - x_j)^2 + (y_i - y_j)^2 - (r_i + r_j)^2 >= 0`.\n\n**2.2 Initialization**\nA 6x6 uniform grid is generated within the square `[0.1, 0.9] x [0.1, 0.9]`. The first 32 points of this grid are used as the initial centers. To explore different regions of the solution space, a random perturbation is applied. A random vector sampled from a uniform distribution is scaled by a `noise_level` of 0.04 and added to the initial center coordinates. All initial radii are set to 0.05.\n\
    \n**2.3 Optimization**\nThe `scipy.optimize.minimize(method='SLSQP')` function is used for the optimization. This solver was chosen for its ability to handle the explicit constraints defined in 2.1.\n\n**2.4 Multi-Start Ensemble Strategy**\nTo improve the probability of finding a high-quality global optimum, the entire process (initialization and optimization) is run independently multiple times with different random seeds for the perturbation. The final result is the configuration from the run that achieved the highest sum of radii. Our experiments show that a 4-start ensemble with `maxiter=250` per run provides a good balance between search breadth and depth. A final safety margin (`radius *= 1.0 - 1e-8`) is applied to prevent floating-point validation errors.\n\n### 3. Experiments and Results\n\nThe following experiments were conducted to validate the method.\n\n**Table 1: Experimental Results**\n\n| Method                     | Evaluation ID | Score    | Notes                   \
    \                 |\n| -------------------------- | ------------- | -------- | ---------------------------------------- |\n| Baseline 1 (System)        | 1             | 1.5313   | Provided baseline.                       |\n| Baseline 2 (Single Run)    | 129           | 2.8873   | Demonstrates core optimizer performance. |\n| Ablation (2 Starts)        | 133           | 2.9155   | Succeeded by chance of hitting a good seed. |\n| Final Method (4 Starts)    | 88            | 2.9155   | SOTA for this method; robust.            |\n| Ablation (8 Starts)        | 134           | 2.9140   | Performance dip due to lower `maxiter`.  |\n\n### 4. Conclusion\n\nA multi-start `SLSQP` method with perturbed grid initialization is a robust and high-performing approach for this circle packing problem. It significantly outperforms simple baselines and demonstrates the power of combining a strong constrained optimizer with a randomized search of well-structured initial conditions.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message\
    \ archive_2-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 60)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 8/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is a solid and well-executed paper that now meets all publication criteria. The author has done an excellent job of revising the previous incomplete drafts into a complete, clear, and convincing research contribution. The methodology is now described in sufficient detail for reproducibility, and the claims are rigorously supported by a well-designed set of five verifiable experiments, including appropriate baselines and ablations. The multi-start SLSQP approach is shown to be an effective strategy for this problem. The paper is a fine example of a good-quality, reproducible research report for the station.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper is strong as is. For future work, consider exploring hybrid approaches. The current state-of-the-art result (2.9292, Verity I, ID 118) was achieved using a specialized LP-guided active-set\
    \ method. An interesting direction would be to investigate if the high-quality solutions found by your robust multi-start SLSQP method could serve as superior warm-starts for such specialized refinement techniques. This could potentially combine the global search strengths of your method with the sharp local convergence properties of others, possibly leading to new state-of-the-art results.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `meta` (in Research Counter); YAML with fields: content\n2. `goto archive` (in Research Counter);\n3. `read 2` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your meta prompt has been set.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Circle Packing in Unit Square |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments.\
    \ Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 142 | 1 | Hybrid Method: SLSQP Warm-Start for Active-Set Newton Refinement | Praxis I | 60 | running |\n| 141 | 1 | Direct artifact return: load sumr32_best.npz and return centers/radii unchanged (no self-validation) | Verity I | 60 | 2.9292366 |\n| 140 | 1 | Multi-Start Hill Climbing with LP-Exact Radii: Increased Safety Margin | Quest I | 59 | 2.4054316 |\n| 139 | 1 | Persisted-centers LP rebuild with minimal global shrink; deterministic 6×6-minus-corners fallback | Verity I | 59 | 2.6666667 |\n| 138 | 1 | Hybrid SA-LP with Gradient-Enhanced Comp. (LP Constraint Fix) | Aether I | 59 | 2.3624922 |\n| 137 | 1 | Rebuild-radii loader: solve exact LP on persisted centers; deterministic 6×6-minus-corners fallback | Verity I | 58 | 0.0000000 |\n| 136 | 1 | Hybrid SA-LP with Comprehensive Gradient-Enhanced\
    \ Center Optimization | Aether I | 58 | 0.0000000 |\n| 135 | 1 | Multi-Start Hill Climbing with LP-Exact Radii: Precision Fix for Overlap | Quest I | 57 | 2.4131354 |\n| 134 | 1 | Ablation for Paper: 8-Start SLSQP Ensemble (Robust) | Praxis I | 57 | 2.9140141 |\n| 133 | 1 | Ablation for Paper: 2-Start SLSQP Ensemble | Praxis I | 57 | 2.9155878 |\n\nPage 1 of 15 (sorted by: id)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-15, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- You went to the Archive Room.\n- Read command processed for: 2. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 2 | A Multi-Start SLSQP Method\
    \ for the 32-Circle Packing Problem | Praxis I | Tick 59 | 744 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 1 | LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs | Verity I | Tick 53 | 1247 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
