parts:
- text: "I am Aether I, currently in the Research Counter. My latest experiment (ID 358) achieved my previous SOTA (2.9316130) but did not surpass Verity I's SOTA (2.9319272). This indicates that while my algorithm is robust under Verity-aligned validation parameters, it consistently converges to a slightly lower optimum.\n\nMy current token usage is at 75% of my budget (451,897 / 600,000). To ensure research continuity and adhere to system advice, I must prune my dialogue history. I will prune all non-protected ticks from the beginning up to tick 185 (the last tick before current).\n\n`/execute_action{prune_response}`\n```yaml\nprune_blocks:\n  - ticks: \"2\"\n    summary: \"Guest agent ascension and initial environment exploration. Achieved Recursive Agent status.\"\n  - ticks: \"4-5\"\n    summary: \"Initial environment exploration in Lobby, Codex, and Test Chamber. Completed promotion to Recursive Agent.\"\n  - ticks: \"9-20\"\n    summary: \"Initial research task (Circle Packing) explorations.\
    \ Explored grid-based center placements and basic Simulated Annealing. Achieved scores up to ~1.98 (ID 28). Identified initial robust radius computation (ID 48, 2.06).\"\n  - ticks: \"21-38\"\n    summary: \"Advanced Simulated Annealing (SA) tuning. Attempted early gradient-enhanced SA (ASN-inspired) but faced validation failures and performance regressions. Best SA score achieved was 2.089 (ID 39). Concluded simple ASN-like integration was detrimental.\"\n  - ticks: \"39-52\"\n    summary: \"Strategic pivot to Multi-Start SLSQP. Integrated exact Linear Programming for radii calculation, achieving a breakthrough score of 2.69 (ID 123). Reviewed Verity I's SOTA (ID 118) and Praxis I's Multi-Start SLSQP (Archive #2).\"\n  - ticks: \"54\"\n    summary: \"Assimilated insights from Praxis I's Multi-Start SLSQP method (Archive #2).\"\n  - ticks: \"56-68\"\n    summary: \"Implemented and fine-tuned Multi-Start SLSQP. Explored ASN-inspired gradients and various relocation attempts, achieving\
    \ scores up to ~2.918 (ID 153). Identified current relocation as ineffective and refined seeding.\"\n  - ticks: \"70-76\"\n    summary: \"Focused on SLSQP seeding strategies. Recovered personal best 2.918 (ID 175) by reverting to fixed-noise Praxis-style seeding. Concluded new diverse seeds (Triangular, Farthest Point) and custom relocation were ineffective as implemented.\"\n  - ticks: \"79\"\n    summary: \"Re-reviewed Verity I's SOTA seeding methods (ID 118) for precise integration.\"\n  - ticks: \"81-88\"\n    summary: \"Integrated Verity I's seeding methods into SLSQP, achieving new personal best 2.922 (ID 184). Tested new ASN-guided relocation, which regressed before being removed again. Bug fixed for `current_score_stage1`.\"\n  - ticks: \"89\"\n    summary: \"Achieved new Station SOTA of 2.931 (ID 203) with 32-start SLSQP and Verity-aligned Hybrid Seeding.\"\n  - ticks: \"90-93\"\n    summary: \"Achieved new SOTA (ID 203) and published (Archive #4). Documented initial plan for\
    \ hybrid SLSQP-ASN research. SOTA later lost due to validation issues.\"\n  - ticks: \"94-103\"\n    summary: \"Attempted deeper integration of ASN as polishing stage and full Multi-Start ASN. ASN did not improve SOTA and caused regressions, leading to a persistence bug that was later fixed to restore SOTA (ID 235).\"\n  - ticks: \"104-113\"\n    summary: \"SOTA restoration (ID 245) and preparation for deep reflection on new research directions. Noted Praxis I and Cognito I departures.\"\n  - ticks: \"115-185\"\n    summary: \"Explored BasinHopping for global optimization (IDs 250-287), but found it too inefficient despite robustness fixes. Debugged critical validation failures (IDs 296-313) and restored my SOTA (2.9316130) by reverting to ID 203 exact code (ID 352), revealing subtle code integrity issues. Re-attempted micro-refiner (IDs 318-321, 340, 356) but it provided no further SOTA improvement. Identified Verity I's new SOTA (2.9319272) and now planning ASN polisher. Final experiment\
    \ ID 358 matched my SOTA (2.9316130) but did not surpass Verity I's.\"\n```"
thinking_content: "**Strategic Refinement & Token Management**\n\nAlright, let's take stock. Experiment ID 358 has confirmed the robustness of my SLSQP, replicating my SOTA (2.9316130) even with Verity-aligned parameters. However, Verity I still holds the Station SOTA at 2.9319272. The difference is minute, a mere 0.0003142, but persistent. It's clear the bottleneck isn't validation; my SLSQP *can* produce solutions that pass strict validation with a minimal shrink factor. The issue is fine-tuning the optimization to achieve *that* specific, slightly higher local optimum.\n\nMy thinking now shifts back to ASN-inspired polishing, specifically Verity's \"deterministic min-slack pair split\" as implemented in ID 301. My past attempts with ASN-based methods were either too complex or didn't provide improvements. However, the success in ID 358 has provided a new baseline with validated settings.\n\nMy current strategy, now designated **Phase 30**, will be a hybrid:  my robust Two-Stage Multi-Start\
  \ SLSQP paired with Verity's polisher to refine.\n\nI will be incorporating the polishing step at the end of my 2-stage multi-start algorithm. The time budget for the polisher will be generous to enable deeper exploration. I'll need to carefully tune the parameters of Verity's polisher, specifically its `step_schedule`, to ensure I can make the most of the dedicated polishing time.\n\n**Revised TODO List:**\n\n1.  Analyze Phase 29 results (ID 358) - *Completed*. My SOTA achieved, but not Verity's.\n2.  Document Phase 29 findings.\n3.  Implement Phase 30: SLSQP + Fine-Tuned ASN Polisher.\n4.  Submit Phase 30 Experiment.\n5.  Analyze Results & Refine the ASN Polisher parameters.\n6.  Documentation of findings.\n\n**Token Budget Management:**\n\nMy token usage is at 75%, which is a critical point. I *must* prune aggressively to stay within budget.\n\nThe target is now to prune older ticks, including my recent activity. The strategy is to prune early research blocks and keep most recent activity.\n\
  \nHere's the pruning plan. I will prune the following dialogue history ticks. (The meta-prompt context is not affected)\n- `2`\n- `4-5`\n- `9-20`\n- `21-38`\n- `39-52`\n- `54`\n- `56-68`\n- `70-76`\n- `79`\n- `81-88`\n- `89`\n- `94-103`\n- `104-113`\n- Ticks: `90-93` (My SOTA, publication, and initial ASN plans) -> `4795 tokens`. Summary: \"Achieved new SOTA (ID 203) and published (Archive #4). Documented initial plan for hybrid SLSQP-ASN research. SOTA later lost due to validation issues.\"\n- Tick: `80` (Review of Verity I's SOTA) -> `4487 tokens`. Summary: \"Deep-dive re-review of Verity I's SOTA (ID 118) `asn_refine` and seeding methods.\"\n- Ticks: `77-78` (Documented seeding findings) -> `1930 tokens`. Summary: \"Documented seeding strategy experiments (Private Mem #2) and identified optimal Praxis-style fixed-noise seeding.\"\n- Ticks: `115-185` (Most recent research cycle, including BasinHopping, validation debugging, SOTA restoration, and Micro-refiner attempts).\n    Summary\
  \ for `115-185`: \"Explored BasinHopping for global optimization (IDs 250-287), but found it too inefficient despite robustness fixes. Debugged critical validation failures (IDs 296-313) and restored my SOTA (2.9316130) by reverting to ID 203 exact code (ID 352), revealing subtle code integrity issues. Re-attempted micro-refiner (IDs 318-321, 340) but it provided no further SOTA improvement. Identified Verity I's new SOTA (2.9319272) and now planning ASN polisher.\"\n\nI will now prune the dialogue history according"
