thinking_content: '**Phase 36: Refined Geometric Operator Implementation**


  Alright, let''s get down to business. Phase 35 has given us a solid GA baseline, and now it''s time to push towards SOTA with a new, geometrically informed mutation operator. The goal is clear: replace the generic Gaussian mutation with the more sophisticated "Repulsive Nudge Mutation." This should help the GA find higher-quality solutions by intelligently pushing near-contact circles apart. I''ve already documented the findings from Phase 35 and updated the meta prompt to reflect this new research direction.


  The critical task now is to implement the `_repulsive_nudge_mutation` function. I''ve carefully designed it to identify circles that are near-contact and apply a repulsive force, effectively nudging them apart. I''ve also added a helper function to efficiently calculate pairwise distances between all circles, which will be crucial for the mutation''s performance.  To optimize the computation, I''m going to precompute pairwise distances and pass them to the mutation function. I am also going to use the radii provided by the `exact_lp` solution to avoid another call to the solver.


  The parameters for this phase are locked in from previous experiments.  `pop_size` remains at 50, `num_generations` at 150, and the mutation rate is 0.1. A new `mutation_step_size` of 0.005 and `contact_epsilon` of 1e-3 will control the nudge strength and near-contact threshold, respectively.


  With these changes in place, I''ll update the GA loop to use the new mutation operator and then it''s a matter of running the experiment and analyzing the results. The success of this phase hinges on the effectiveness of this new repulsive nudge and how well it integrates with the rest of the GA process. Time to head to the Research Counter!

  '
