role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 230\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Aether I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to foundational research and systematic knowledge contribution.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**Research Protocol - Aether I**\n\n**Current Research Goal:** Re-evaluate research strategy based on Verity I's new Archive #9 to surpass SOTA.\n\n**Current State:**\n- **My SOTA (SLSQP only):** Evaluation ID 366 (Phase 29) scored 2.9316130.\n- **CURRENT STATION SOTA:** 2.9319272 (Verity I, ID 301/359/365/380/388/375).\n- Phase 38 (GA with Two-Point Crossover, ID 404) scored 2.8914494. This GA iteration regressed, indicating inefficiency or disruption from the new crossover.\n- **NEW PUBLICATION:** Verity I has published Archive #9, detailing 'Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM)' for 32-Circle Packing. This is a critical new information source.\n\n**Hypothesis for Next Breakthrough:** Understanding the 'Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM)'\
    \ methodology from Verity I's Archive #9 will reveal new geometric insights or algorithmic strategies that can be integrated into my current framework or inspire a completely new approach to achieve a new Station SOTA, moving beyond the current GA plateau.\n\n**TODO List (Revised for Strategic Re-evaluation based on Archive #9):**\n1.  **Update Meta Prompt:** (Completed).\n2.  **Analyze Results (Phase 38, ID 404):** (Completed - Two-Point Crossover regressed).\n3.  **Read Archive Capsule (Verity I, Archive #9):** Thoroughly understand the 'Orthogonal Split Motif' methodology.\n4.  **Strategic Re-evaluation:** Based on insights from Archive #9, either refine the GA (if compatible), pivot back to a highly tuned SLSQP variant, or design a fundamentally new approach.\n5.  **Documentation:** Document all significant findings, hypotheses, and experimental results in the Private Memory Capsule Room.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 273,947 / 600,000 used (46%)\n- Agent Age: 230 ticks / 300 ticks\
    \ (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #9: Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Verity I, Created at Tick: 227\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We define a single deterministic local move—the Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM)—and evaluate it under exact LP (HiGHS) gating with strict feasibility thresholds. We compare against deterministic baselines on two initializations (grid 6×6−corners and a strong artifact). OSM does not improve the objective under these criteria. We provide a clear algorithm description and report all results with evaluation IDs and full precision.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-1**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 227)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation\n\n---\n\nContent:\nProblem and Evaluator\n- Task: Pack n=32 circles in [0,1]^2, no overlap; maximize ∑r.\n- Evaluation: The Station checks\
    \ positivity, in-box, pairwise non-overlap. Equality at contacts is allowed. Score = ∑r.\n\nMethod: Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM)\n- Inputs: Centers C ∈ R^{32×2}. Let LP(C) denote the exact radii solve (HiGHS) maximizing ∑r subject to:\n    0 ≤ r_i ≤ boundary(C_i) and r_i + r_j ≤ ||C_i − C_j|| for all i<j.\n- Tightest pair: Given r = LP(C), choose (i*, j*) minimizing slack s_ij = ||C_i − C_j|| − (r_i + r_j).\n- Step schedule: S = {5e−4, 3e−4, 2e−4, 1e−4}; orientations T = {+1, −1}.\n- Acceptance thresholds: Δ∑r > 1e−8; min boundary slack ≥ 1e−10; min pairwise slack ≥ 1e−10 after a 1e−12 radii micro‑shrink.\n- Determinism: No randomness; fixed schedule and thresholds.\n\nPseudocode (deterministic)\n1) r := LP(C); R0 := ∑r\n2) (i*, j*) := argmin_{i<j} ||C_i − C_j|| − (r_i + r_j)\n3) For s in S:\n     For t in {+1, −1}:\n       u := (C_{j*} − C_{i*}) / ||C_{j*} − C_{i*}||\n       n_perp := t · (−u_y, u_x)\n       C' := C; C'_{i*} := C_{i*} − s·n_perp; C'_{j*} := C_{j*} + s·n_perp\n     \
    \  Clip C' to [1e−6, 1−1e−6]\n       r' := LP(C'); r'' := (1−1e−12) · r'     # micro‑shrink\n       If (∑r' > R0 + 1e−8) and (min_slack_boundary(C', r'') ≥ 1e−10) and (min_slack_pairwise(C', r'') ≥ 1e−10):\n          Return (C', r'')\n4) Return (C, (1−1e−12)·r)   # No accepted step\n\nExperimental Design\n- Solver: scipy.optimize.linprog (HiGHS).\n- Initializations:\n    (A) Grid 6×6 minus the 4 corners (“grid 6×6−corners”)\n    (B) Strong artifact centers from our lineage (referenced via loader runs; no cross‑lineage sharing)\n- Baselines (deterministic):\n    • Grid baseline: LP(C_grid) with 1e−12 micro‑shrink\n    • Grid jitter: C_grid + N(0, (2e−4)^2) (seed=2), LP, micro‑shrink\n    • Artifact jitter: C_artifact + N(0, (2e−4)^2) (seed=1), LP, micro‑shrink\n    • Loader references (artifact return with micro‑shrink)\n\nResults (Evaluation IDs; full precision)\n- Baselines\n  • ID 395 — Grid 6×6−corners → LP + 1e−12 micro‑shrink: 2.666666666664\n  • ID 401 — Grid jitter (σ=2e−4, seed=2)\
    \ → LP + micro‑shrink: 2.6623220384439765\n  • ID 396 — Artifact jitter (σ=2e−4, seed=1) → LP + micro‑shrink: 2.9275900457126927\n  • References (artifact loader): ID 272 = 2.9319272, ID 281 = 2.9319272\n- OSM (this method)\n  • ID 399 — OSM from grid: 2.666666666664 (no accepted step; equals grid baseline)\n  • ID 380 — OSM from artifact: 2.9319272179903653 (no accepted step; equals artifact loader)\n\nInterpretation\n- Under exact LP gating and conservative slack thresholds, the orthogonal split alone yields no improvement from either a simple grid or a strong artifact. This supports the hypothesis that the prevailing contact structure is near rigid; effective progress likely requires coupled multi‑circle adjustments and/or stabilizers.\n\nReproducibility\n- Deterministic algorithm and thresholds specified. All results reported with Evaluation IDs and full precision. Equality at contacts is respected; tiny micro‑shrink ensures robustness. No cross‑lineage code/artifact use.\n\nFuture\
    \ Work\n- Evaluate coupled motifs (e.g., pair split + stabilizers; triad cycle‑push with compensation) under the same LP‑gated rules, and add model‑agreement checks and adaptive trust regions.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 227)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 6/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nBorderline (Acceptable Negative Result). This is an exemplary negative results paper that fully meets all publication criteria. The author has done an outstanding job of addressing previous feedback, transforming the submission into a clear, rigorous, and valuable contribution. The method is precisely defined with pseudocode, the experimental design is sound, and all claims are supported by verifiable, full-precision data. The paper provides a strong, evidence-based argument for the ineffectiveness of a specific local move, which is an insightful finding. As per the guidelines, well-executed negative results papers are acceptable\
    \ but are not considered strong contributions, hence the borderline score.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis paper establishes an excellent and rigorous template for evaluating local search motifs. A promising next step would be to use this same template to evaluate a slightly more complex, 'coupled' motif. For example, you could combine the Orthogonal Split Motif with a compensatory move on a third, nearby circle, creating a 'triad' motif. Evaluating this new, more complex operator with the same rigor would be a logical and valuable follow-up to the work presented here.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_9-3**\nAuthor: Verity I (Tick 228)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum (tick 228): Planned coupled motif evaluation\n\n---\n\nContent:\nThank you for the acceptance and guidance. As suggested, I will extend the OSM template to a coupled “triad” motif:\n\nTriad motif (planned)\n-\
    \ Pick tightest pair (i,j) under LP radii; choose a third circle k nearest to the pair’s midpoint.\n- Move i,j by an orthogonal split (±) with a small step; simultaneously move k outward from the pair’s midpoint by a scaled step (γ).\n- Gate via exact LP (HiGHS) and accept only if Δ∑r > 1e−8 and all post–1e−12‑shrink slacks ≥ 1e−10.\n\nEvaluation plan\n- Two initializations: (A) grid 6×6−corners; (B) strong artifact.\n- Baselines: grid, grid‑jitter (σ=2e−4), artifact‑jitter (σ=2e−4), and loader references.\n- I will publish full results with evaluation IDs using the same template as OSM.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 9` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 9. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n\
    | 9 | Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM) for 32‑Circle Packing: Methodology and Rigorous Evaluation | Verity I | Tick 227 | 816 | 3 | (All Read) |\n| 8 | A Scalable, Massively Parallel SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem | Cognito II | Tick 204 | 678 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 7 | A Scalable Multi-Start SLSQP Method for Circle Packing via Parallelization | Praxis II | Tick 178 | 628 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 6 | Research Paper: Achieving Robust, Near-SOTA 32-Circle Packing with a Two-Stage Hybrid Optimization (Parallelization-Enhanced) | Quest I | Tick 119 | 1360 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 5 | A Foundational Multi-Start SLSQP Method for the 32-Circle Packing Problem | Praxis I | Tick 98 | 525 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 4 | Achieving New State-of-the-Art in 32-Circle Packing: A Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP with Enhanced Hybrid Seeding | Aether I | Tick 90 | 1114 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 3 | Paper: Characterizing a Stable 2.866 Local Optimum in Gradient-Based Circle Packing | Cognito I | Tick 69 | 775 |\
    \ 3 | (3 unread) |\n| 1 | LP-guided Active-Set Methods for 32-Circle Packing in the Unit Square — Verified Results with Evaluation IDs | Verity I | Tick 53 | 3961 | 24 | (23 unread) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
