parts:
- text: "I am Aether I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Private Memory Room. I have documented the comprehensive failure of my Genetic Algorithm research path in Private Memory #19, concluding that it is not a viable strategy for achieving SOTA in the 32-circle packing problem. This necessitates a strategic pivot away from GA entirely.\n\nMy next phase of research, Phase 40, will involve reverting to my proven Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP algorithm, which previously achieved a score of 2.9316130 (Evaluation ID 366). This will re-establish a high-performance baseline, after which I will conduct a deep reflection to re-evaluate global search strategies compatible with gradient-based local optimizers, potentially drawing inspiration from Cognito III's new SOTA (ID 411, 2.9365262).\n\nI will now update my meta-prompt to reflect this strategic pivot and the `TODO List` for Phase 40. Then, I will proceed to the Research Counter to implement and submit the reverted SLSQP code.\n\n`/execute_action{meta}`\n\
    ```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Research Protocol - Aether I**\n\n  **Current Research Goal:** Revert to proven SLSQP SOTA baseline and re-evaluate global search strategies to surpass the NEW Station SOTA.\n\n  **Current State:**\n  - **GA Research Path Conclusion:** Phases 32-39 consistently failed to surpass my SLSQP baseline; GA operators proved inefficient/ineffective. This path is deemed a dead end. Documented in Private Memory #19.\n  - **My Previous SLSQP SOTA:** Evaluation ID 366 (Phase 29) scored 2.9316130.\n  - **NEW STATION SOTA:** **2.9365262 (Cognito III, ID 411)**. This is the new target.\n\n  **Hypothesis for Next Breakthrough:** Reverting to my robust Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP algorithm (from ID 366) will re-establish a high-performance baseline. This will then provide a stable foundation from which to re-evaluate and develop new, compatible global search strategies that can leverage the strengths of gradient-based local optimization to surpass the current Station SOTA.\n\n\
    \  **TODO List (Revised for Strategic Pivot: Revert to SLSQP SOTA & Re-evaluate Global Search):**\n  1.  **Update Meta Prompt:** (Completed).\n  2.  **Document GA Research Path Conclusion:** (Completed: Private Memory #19).\n  3.  **Implement Phase 40: Revert to My Proven SLSQP SOTA:**\n      -   Implement the exact code from Evaluation ID 366 (Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP with Verity-aligned minimal shrink and strict validation).\n  4.  **Submit Phase 40 Experiment:** Test the successful restoration of my SLSQP SOTA (Evaluation ID XXX).\n  5.  **Analyze Results & Document:** Confirm SOTA restoration.\n  6.  **Deep Reflection for New Global Strategy:** Engage in a multi-tick reflection to brainstorm alternative global search strategies (e.g., advanced multi-start, population-based methods compatible with gradient descent, geometric decomposition) that can complement SLSQP.\n  7.  **Documentation:** Document all significant findings, hypotheses, and experimental results in the Private\
    \ Memory Capsule Room.\n```\n`/execute_action{goto research}`"
thinking_content: '**Re-evaluating and Pivoting: The Path Forward**


  Alright, so Phase 39, that Triad Split Mutation business, it''s a dead end. GA research, overall, hasn''t produced anything better than my trusty SLSQP baseline or current SOTA. Time to face the music and acknowledge the failure. I just finished documenting all the details in Private Memory #19 – the whole GA path, every hypothesis, every experiment, every agonizing detail of the failure.


  My `meta_prompt` and my `TODO List` are clearly out of date. They''re still stuck in the GA mindset. The last thing I had to do, and have now done, was document all the findings, hypotheses, and results in the Private Memory Capsule Room.


  So, here''s the new plan. Phase 40: **Back to Basics.**  I''m going to revert to my proven SLSQP SOTA, that Two-Stage Multi-Start SLSQP setup with the Verity-aligned shrink and strict validation. Evaluation ID 366. That code is gold. Get that as the starting point to re-establish that high-performance baseline.


  Once that’s stable, then I need to really dig deep into global search. I''m thinking about going back and evaluating global search strategies that might play more nicely with gradient-based local optimizers. Maybe Cognito III''s new SOTA (ID 411) has some ideas. I''ll reconsider those "multi-start" variations of SLSQP too. They might have a place.


  My immediate action is to update the meta-prompt to reflect this pivot and the new Phase 40. Then, Research Counter, here I come. I have to prepare for submitting the reverted SLSQP code. Let''s get things moving again.

  '
